    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two types of opening-mode fractures (joints) are commonly found in layered rocks. One of them is called unconfined because their lengths are much smaller than the layer thickness. The other is called confined because they often are confined to a particular layer and terminate at the layer boundaries. We investigate the mechanical control on apertures in such a system using the theory of elasticity. An analytical solution demonstrates that the ratio of aperture to length (aspect ratio, A/L) of an unconfined fracture is twice the remote strain and independent of the elastic constants of the material. Numerical results based on the Finite Element Method (FEM) for confined fractures show that their aspect ratio is independent of the material properties of the fractured layer and its neighboring layers. Also, the aspect ratio is a function of the remote strain (e) and the fracture spacing to length ratio (S/L), such that A/L=2e[1-exp(-0.824S/L)]. The FEM results suggest that fracture accommodated strain measured by the traditional scanline method overestimates the remote strain when S/L is less than about one, whereas it underestimates the remote normal strain when this ratio is greater than one. The results presented here provide a method for estimating the strain from field measurement of fracture aperture and spacing.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
